#!/usr/bin/env bash

lord --verbose << "EOF"

What follows is a history of several past prompts. Read all. Only do the final one.

# PROMPT 1

Ok so, I'm using fontforge with a bunch of ancient fonts created by different people.
They're all in fonts/.

Some of these fonts put Paleo Hebrew in the Hebrew block.
Others put it in the phoenician block.
Others encode it with ascii latin letters.
You can tell which does which by reading master.tex.

I want you to help me make these fonts more uniform in how they work.

Create a new directory fonts2/.

Then go through each of the semitic or egyptian fonts in fonts/
and help me uniformize them into a new font in fonts2/

The new fonts should have:

1. Metadata and font name matching the current filenames.
2. Sizes appropriately scaled relative to one another.
   You can find the rough relative scales in master.tex
   where we build the alphabet history page.
3. Sizes appropriately scaled relative to Noto Sans.
4. All fonts in fonts2 should be empty, with zero glyphs
   in them except for the Hebrew unicode block.
   The letter aleph א should be rendered via each font
   in fonts2 as either proto-sinaitic aleph, or paleo-hebrew
   aleph, or whatever. You'll need to figure out where each
   font stores its interesting glyphs in order to accomplish
   this. Remember: some of them store the interesting glyphs
   in the ascii letter range!
5. In cases where a font provides multiple representations
   of a given glyph, e.g. some proto-sinaitic fonts for
   which 'a' and 'A' give different shapes of ancient aleph,
   I want those fonts split out into TWO different fonts
   in the fonts2 directory.

Do that now, and then write me a bash script that uses pango-view
to test all the fonts in fonts2, and verify that they do indeed
render the standard Hebrew unicode range into the glyphs in question.

# PROMPT 2

Take all the fonts in fonts2, and turn each one into an ascii representation
of the same font, using the transliteration below:

a    א
b,v  ב
g    ג
d    ד
e,h  ה
w    ו
z    ז
x,H  ח
T,0  ט
i,j,y י
k  כ
K  ך
l  ל
m  מ
M  ם
n  נ
N  ן
S  ס
o,A ע
p  פ
P  ף
c  צ
C  ץ
q  ק
r  ר
s  ש
t  ת

whenever a given upper or lowercase ascii letter is not used in the transliteration,
give it a meaningful value that reflects the choices made above.

By doing this, create an entire fonts-ascii directory with each of the fonts in fonts2,
but as an ascii representation.

# PROMPT 3

Fix the spacing between the glyphs in the ascii fonts in fonts-ascii/.
They're overlapping each other in ways that I expect are programmatically fixable.

Also normalize their sizes so they roughly match Noto Sans of any given point size.

# PROMPT 4

Go through fonts-ascii/ and use the file names and glyph shapes to:
1. Add estimated years to the file names, e.g., -15c- or -13c- for
   the 15th and 13th centuries bc respectively.
2. Rename the proto-sinaitic 13th century fonts and izbet-sartah ones to
   proto-canaanite
3. Systematize the file names in any other ways you think would be valuable,
   for example, in order to have them alphabetically sort into reverse
   chronological order.
4. Generate one big png of them all rendering the same letter sequence
   that shows all their letters.

# PROMPT 5

Invert the numerical prefixes on the fonts-ascii/* filenames so they sort from oldest to newest. Keep the centuries.


# PROMPT 6

1. Give the fonts in fonts-ascii/ more useful Family names for fontconfig.
They don't have to match the filenames exactly, they should be useful.
Also add any metadata that fonts typically contain.

2. Check the dates and filenames against the information here:
    https://www.bibleplaces.com/paleo_hebrew_fonts

Compare the zip files linked on that page to the files in fonts-ascii,
and use that information as needed to give the fonts more descriptive
names, and more accurate names. For example, the wadi-el-hol and serabit
parts of those filenames may not be entirely accurate, so use the glyph
shapes if necessary as part of your chronology and naming system.

3. Finally, regenerate the fonts-ascii visualization page, but this time
make it into a table with a column for each letter, in hebrew alphabet order.


# PROMPT 7

Compare fonts-ascii to the fonts in fonts/ and fonts2/, and use the
information you find there and in master.tex to fix up the fonts in
fonts-ascii, specifically as regards:

1. incorrectly mapped dalet vs samekh in certain proto-sinaitic fonts.
2. egyptian-noto-sans ascii becomes incorrectly mapped starting at tsade
    and then continues wrong for q,r,s,t.
3. ugaritic and serabit el khadem don't need to be in the font comparison table pdf
4. we need to decide on a transliteration once and for all. I want o to map to ayin, since
   it's historically the ancestor of the letter o. I think he can be e and heth can be h,
   since that's the ancestry there. But I also want certain letters to be accessible through
   their non-ascii descendant letters. Russian с should map to samekh, Greek σ should be samekh too.
   Russian п and Greek π should map to פ in every font of fonts-ascii. Similarly, Θ and θ should
   map to teth, given the obvious similarity ancestry wise. All hebrew letters should map to
   the correct glyphs in each font. All ascii letters should map to the ancestor of that letter
   as described above (e.g., o is ayin, a is aleph, e is he, h is heth, etc.) Similarly,
   Greek Η and η should map to Heth, since Heth is their ancestor. Russian И comes from Eta
   and should therefore map to Heth. Russian Ш and ш should map to the glyph for Shin.
   š and ʃ should map to shin too. Every hebrew letter should be accessible via at least
   one ascii character, but it may be accessible from many more as well. The master key
   to represent each hebrew letter is its ascii letter.

First carefully consider the transliteration, make a big master dict for it in bin/
that will define a map from (a) a configurable subset of unicode, the keys, to
(b) the smaller number of letters represented by a given font, in this case, each
font is just representing the hebrew alphabet, but in various ancient versions.
Once the value space is set, the key space can be arbitrarily large and multilingual.
So define the mappings above first, in a file called (say) bin/alphabet, and then
use that script as your guide to fix up all the fonts in fonts-ascii/. Remember,
you may need to use information in master.tex and in fonts/ and fonts2/ to help you too.
Finally regenerate the full pdf table of the alphabet, and move the script you used to
do so into bin/, and name it something simple and memorable.

Go do that now, and don't stop until you're confident that you've mapped
all the glyphs in fonts-ascii correctly. Then delete fonts2, and rename
fonts-ascii to fonts-master, and fix up any scripts that used to use the
term fonts-ascii.

# PROMPT 8

1. Remove the terms 'ascii' and upper/lower from the font filenames in fonts-master, and from their fontconfig family names if necessary.
2. Change the fonts in fonts-ascii/ currently named wadi-el-hol and serabit to just proto sinaitic 15th century or something like that.
3. Rename 03-bc15c-proto-sinaitic-serabit-el-khadim-ascii.ttf and 03-bc15c-proto-sinaitic-serabit-el-khadim-bold-ascii.ttf so that the one that's currently bold is just the default name, and the other one is that with -light in the name somewhere.
4. Include the non-light version of the above in the chart, but not the light one. Still don't include ugaritic.
5. After you've done all that, use the svgs in the fonts-proto-sinaitic-{serabit,wadi-el-hol,generic} directories to make two new fonts, resembling the wadi el hol inscriptions and the serabit el khadem inscriptions. Substitute existing 15th c glyphs whenever you need to, but try to keep the ones in those directories so we have letters that resemble the inscriptions in those fonts. Make sure the glyphs are all sensibly sized like the other fonts.

Do that now, and don't stop til you're done.

# PROMPT 9

1. Fix up the tofu problems in the serabit and wadi-el-hol proto-sinaitic fonts by examining the glyphs and how they rendered in the pdf. 
2. Fix up the serabit and wadi-el-hol fonts to make their weight more bold so that it matches the other proto-sinatic fonts bold weight.
3. Remove the word variant from the font names.
4. Substitute glyphs from nearby proto sinaitic fonts whenever wadi or serabit are missing a glyph. Try to use different glyphs when both are missing one.
5. Whenever ANY of the fonts in fonts-master are missing a glyph, substitute a nearby glyph, and write a README in the fonts-master directory saying which ones are substituted.
6. Make the egyptian row at the top bold.


# PROMPT 9
# (CURRENT PROMPT)

You've already done most of the work for this, but you crashed, so I want you to finish up.

Here's the prompt.

1. Glyphs:

Your serabit, wadi, and generic proto sinaitic fonts have:
- An incorrect teth

Your serabit and wadi proto sinaitic fonts have:
- Strange hollow outlined qoph
- Strange hollow outlined ayin
- Strange hollow outlined zayin

Serabit has:
- An incorrect shin

Egyptian has:
- Two pe variants, delete the second because it's getting interpreted as tsade and messing up everything after that.

Wadi should be before serabit. Right after Egypt.

Wadi has an incorrect:
- Kaph
- Lamed
- Samekh
- Tsade
- Qoph
- Nun

2. Serabit and wadi belong before the other proto sinaitic fonts. Update filenames and Familieis as needed with the following info:

    The two earliest corpora of alphabetic inscriptions are generally dated as follows:
    Corpus	Most likely date	Conservative scholarly range
    Wadi el-Hol	ca. 1900–1800 BCE	ca. 1950–1800 BCE
    Serabit el-Khadim (Proto-Sinaitic)	ca. 1850–1550 BCE (with many inscriptions clustering around the 18th century BCE)	ca. 1900–1500 BCE

3. Make the egyptian row at the top slightly more bold without making it as light as it was before, if possible. But focus on the other problems first.
4. Using everything you've learned about my preferences from the above prompts, fix up ./XCompose
5. Rename fonts-master/08-bc10c-phoenician-noto-sans.ttf by replacing phoenician with paleo-hebrew
6. Don't call isaiah scroll paleo-hebrew in the fonts dir, call it dead-sea-scrolls or whatever.

EOF

